WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.547
So, now that I'm in quarantine,

00:00:01.547 --> 00:00:04.862
I have basically this one hobby,
which is going for walks.

00:00:04.862 --> 00:00:08.723
I do everything on walks,
I listen to podcasts, I take meetings.

00:00:08.723 --> 00:00:10.866
There's just one thing
that's really hard to do on a walk

00:00:10.866 --> 00:00:13.062
and that's reading a research paper.

00:00:13.062 --> 00:00:16.904
Or so I thought until I learned about
a new project that my colleague, Kaz Sato,

00:00:16.904 --> 00:00:18.961
who's based in Japan, is working on.

00:00:18.961 --> 00:00:22.115
He used machine learning
to transform PDFs into audiobooks.

00:00:22.115 --> 00:00:24.262
Today, I'll show you
how to do the same thing,

00:00:24.262 --> 00:00:26.519
how to use computer vision
and text-to-speech

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:28.431
to turn your own PDFs into audiobooks.

00:00:28.431 --> 00:00:29.726
Let's get started.

00:00:29.726 --> 00:00:31.994
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:33.994 --> 00:00:37.219
So first I decided to chat with Kaz
to see how he built his project.

00:00:37.881 --> 00:00:41.046
I have converted the PDF books
into the text file

00:00:41.046 --> 00:00:44.694
by using the Vision API OCR feature.

00:00:44.694 --> 00:00:50.986
I have used here Auto ML Tables
to understand the layout of the document.

00:00:50.986 --> 00:00:54.472
And then I have used
the Text-to-Speech API for converting

00:00:54.472 --> 00:00:57.787
the pre-processed text
into the .mp3 audio file.

00:00:57.787 --> 00:01:00.176
I wanted to build
my own version of Kaz's project

00:01:00.176 --> 00:01:02.299
that would convert
research papers into audiobooks.

00:01:02.299 --> 00:01:04.535
So I'm going to copy most
of Kaz's architecture.

00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:07.270
First, like Kaz, I'll create
a Google Cloud Storage bucket

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.366
where all the PDFs
I want to be converted will be stored.

00:01:10.366 --> 00:01:13.362
Next, I'll use the Vision API
to extract text from those PDFs,

00:01:13.362 --> 00:01:15.845
which I'll later convert
into spoken words.

00:01:15.845 --> 00:01:19.220
I'll use Kaz's code which calls
the Vision API from Python.

00:01:21.711 --> 00:01:25.074
Here's what the output looks like
when you pass a PDF through the API.

00:01:26.424 --> 00:01:28.743
As you can see, it contains
both the raw text

00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:32.545
but also the X, Y coordinates
of the characters.

00:01:33.283 --> 00:01:35.958
So Kaz set up this project
in a really nice way.

00:01:35.958 --> 00:01:38.854
He used a Cloud Function, which is a way
of running a tiny bit of code

00:01:38.854 --> 00:01:40.579
in the cloud when something happens.

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:43.870
In our case, we'll set it up so that
uploading a PDF to cloud storage

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.661
triggers the cloud function that kicks off
our audiobook-making pipeline.

00:01:47.661 --> 00:01:51.351
I'm going to create a cloud function, too,
and copy Kaz's code into it.

00:01:55.065 --> 00:01:58.463
After we've converted the PDFs into text,
we still need to decide

00:01:58.463 --> 00:02:01.519
which parts of the PDF text we want
to include in the audiobook.

00:02:01.519 --> 00:02:04.839
For example, we probably want
the body text and the title,

00:02:04.839 --> 00:02:07.350
but we probably don't care about
things like page numbers

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.725
or references or image captions.

00:02:09.725 --> 00:02:12.512
So this is where our custom
machine-learning model comes in

00:02:12.512 --> 00:02:15.720
to do what Kaz calls
remove the garbage text.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.995
The Auto ML Tables model
can detect whether

00:02:18.995 --> 00:02:21.588
a text could be a garbage text,

00:02:21.588 --> 00:02:23.766
such as the page headers 
or the page numbers,

00:02:23.766 --> 00:02:29.389
or the body text, or the other small labels
you can find in the diagrams.

00:02:29.389 --> 00:02:32.059
So to handle the problem
of identifying the garbage text,

00:02:32.059 --> 00:02:36.123
Kaz built a custom machine-learning model
using a tool called Auto ML Tables.

00:02:36.123 --> 00:02:38.526
To train that model,
he collected all this information

00:02:38.526 --> 00:02:41.219
about the size of the text
on the page and its position.

00:02:41.587 --> 00:02:44.629
But in order to do that, Kaz had
to manually label a lot of PDFs,

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:46.870
marking the good text
from the garbage text.

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:49.181
It took three hours for each book,

00:02:49.181 --> 00:02:52.506
and for the initial five books,

00:02:52.506 --> 00:02:55.760
it took like two days.

00:02:57.124 --> 00:02:58.585
That's 15 hours. (laughs)

00:02:58.585 --> 00:03:02.026
I could do that because I was back
in my parent's home,

00:03:02.026 --> 00:03:05.077
and I had nothing to do
except for this one.

00:03:05.077 --> 00:03:07.317
So in order to avoid
labeling data myself,

00:03:07.317 --> 00:03:09.093
I'm going to use a little cheat here.

00:03:09.093 --> 00:03:12.139
Instead of building a custom model,
I'll just look at all the font sizes

00:03:12.139 --> 00:03:14.332
and whatever font size
is most frequently used,

00:03:14.332 --> 00:03:16.423
I'm just going to assume
that's the body text

00:03:16.423 --> 00:03:18.587
and that should go into the audiobook.

00:03:18.587 --> 00:03:21.062
If you want more details
about my and Kaz's approach,

00:03:21.062 --> 00:03:23.588
make sure you check out
the blog post linked below.

00:03:23.588 --> 00:03:26.629
And now for the fun part:
generating computer voices!

00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:28.264
<i>(computer voice 1) I'll take it from here.</i>

00:03:28.264 --> 00:03:34.053
<i>Google Cloud Text-to-Speech supports
over 220 voices in 40+ languages.</i>

00:03:34.053 --> 00:03:36.177
Let's try another one.

00:03:36.177 --> 00:03:38.077
<i>(computer voice 2) I am your narrator.</i>

00:03:38.974 --> 00:03:40.926
<i>(computer voice 3) I am your narrator.</i>

00:03:41.806 --> 00:03:43.760
<i>(computer voice 4) I am your narrator.</i>

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.911
<i>(computer voice 5) I am your narrator.</i>

00:03:45.911 --> 00:03:47.600
<i>(computer voice 6) I am your narrator.</i>

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.869
Perfect. Now that I've chosen
my favorite voice,

00:03:49.869 --> 00:03:50.975
I can update the codes,

00:03:50.975 --> 00:03:55.311
that when I upload a PDF, an audiobook
will automatically be generated.

00:03:55.311 --> 00:03:57.347
Let's take a look at what it sounds like.

00:03:57.347 --> 00:04:00.061
<i>(computer voice 1) A promising path
towards autoformalization</i>

00:04:00.061 --> 00:04:02.288
<i>in general artificial intelligence.</i>

00:04:03.156 --> 00:04:05.403
<i>(computer voice 2) Christian Szegedy.</i>

00:04:05.403 --> 00:04:08.506
<i>Today, AI systems are able
to learn solving tasks</i>

00:04:08.506 --> 00:04:10.101
<i>that used to be thought of taking</i>

00:04:10.101 --> 00:04:12.833
<i>uniquely human capabilities,
until recently.</i>

00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:14.126
(Dale) Thanks for watching.

00:04:14.126 --> 00:04:16.225
Let me know in the comments
what you want to see me build.

00:04:16.225 --> 00:04:17.589
See you next time.

